>> title: Seared Halloumi
>> category: Fried & Crispy
>> tags: halloumi, cheese, skillet, vegetarian, hot honey
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: seared halloumi, fried halloumi, grilled halloumi, halloumi slabs, hellim, saganaki-style halloumi
>> pairs-with: tabbouleh, hummus
>> servings: 4
>> time: 20 min
>> slack: unforgiving — halloumi is only itself hot; ten minutes on a cold plate and it sets to squeaking rubber that reheating will not undo
>> step.1: rinse the brine off, dry hard
>> step.2: sear 90 sec a face in a dry pan
>> step.3: stir the hot honey
>> step.4: spoon over and eat it now

Rinse @halloumi{9%oz}(250 g) under cold water to take the brine off the surface, cut it into 1/2-in slabs and press them dry between towels until the cloth comes away clean. Halloumi is packed in salt water and that water is the reason a slab spits, slides and refuses to colour.

Sear @&(~1)dried halloumi{} in a **dry** #skillet{} over medium-high, ~sear{90%sec} a face, without touching it. No oil, no butter: the cheese has enough fat of its own and it renders out in the first twenty seconds, so anything you add just fries in a puddle. Half-inch slabs, not thin ones — thin halloumi is done through before the face browns, and a browned face over a molten middle is the whole point.

Stir @honey{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @lemon juice{2%tsp}(10 mL), @red chile flakes{1/2%tsp} and @dried oregano{1/2%tsp} in a small bowl.

Spoon @&(~1)hot honey{} over @&(~2)seared halloumi{} and take it to the table immediately. This is the one thing on the counter that cannot wait: halloumi holds its shape because its proteins were heated in the making, and as it cools those same proteins tighten back to the squeak it is famous for.
